+/// The unit a client sees `modified` in: every S3 surface reports whole seconds (the
+/// `Last-Modified` header and a listing's `<LastModified>`, #767). [`overwrite_modified`]
+/// steps in this unit, so the step it takes is one a client can see.
+const MODIFIED_WIRE_UNIT_MILLIS: u64 = 1_000;
+
+/// How far a superseded generation's `modified` may be ahead of the publishing clock and
+/// still be ordered after: one day. Further ahead it is not skew but a wrong reading (a
+/// gateway that booted with a wrong date, a corrupt value). Ordering after it would carry
+/// it to every later generation of the file, so [`overwrite_modified`] lets the publishing
+/// clock replace it instead, and the file heals at its next overwrite, as it did before
+/// #768. A day keeps #768's case (a gateway an hour fast) well inside the rule, and it
+/// also caps how far a burst of overwrites can run a file ahead of the clock (to a day
+/// plus the one step past it).
+const MODIFIED_MAX_LEAD_MILLIS: u64 = 24 * 60 * 60 * 1_000;
+
+/// The `modified` an **overwrite** commits, given the superseded generation's stamp
+/// (`prior`) and the publisher's (`published`): `max(published, the start of the next
+/// whole second after prior)`. So across overwrites that each record a time, and within
+/// the bounds listed below, a file's last-modified time never goes backwards, and every
+/// overwrite shows a later `Last-Modified` than the generation it replaced (#768).
+///
+/// Each gateway of an active/active fleet stamps from its own clock, so a prior written by
+/// a gateway whose clock runs fast would otherwise be followed by an earlier stamp on newer
+/// content, and a sync tool comparing size and `Last-Modified` would skip a same-size
+/// overwrite. The step is to the next whole second, not the next millisecond, because every
+/// S3 surface reports whole seconds (`MODIFIED_WIRE_UNIT_MILLIS`): a 1 ms step lands in
+/// the prior's own second 999 times in 1000, which a client sees as no change at all. The
+/// prior stamp is the one this commit supersedes: both callers CAS on the exact `prior`
+/// bytes, so the ordering is against the generation actually replaced.
+///
+/// This reads no clock. `published` is the publishing gateway's wall-clock reading (its
+/// `now_millis`, the clock that owns the publication-time lifecycle), and `prior` is a
+/// value the same kind of clock wrote; this only orders one against the other (ADR-0009).
+///
+/// - `published == None` → `None`: a generation published with no time records none. The
+///   chain resets there, and the next stamped overwrite starts from its own clock, because
+///   carrying the prior's time forward (or inventing one) would put a time on content its
+///   publisher never stamped. Both gateway PUT paths always stamp one; only core's API can
+///   publish without it.
+/// - `prior == None` (a legacy record, ADR-0047) → the publisher's stamp stands.
+/// - `prior` more than `MODIFIED_MAX_LEAD_MILLIS` (one day) ahead of `published` → the
+///   publisher's stamp stands: a wrong reading is not carried forward for good. This is the
+///   one case where `modified` can still go backwards across stamped overwrites. It cannot
+///   tell a prior that is too far ahead from a publishing clock that is too far behind;
+///   either way the publishing clock wins, as it did before #768, and it is logged.
+/// - The step saturates: a prior at or near `u64::MAX` (decodable, ADR-0045) commits
+///   `u64::MAX`, never a wrapped value.
+///
+/// Cost: a file overwritten more than once a second runs ahead of the clock by about
+/// (overwrites − elapsed seconds). The lead drains once writes slow down, and the one-day
+/// bound caps it.
+///
+/// Every path that publishes content **over** a prior generation must call this — today
+/// [`commit_chunk_map_superseding`] and [`commit_chunk_map_superseding_leased`]. The multipart
+/// Complete flip (#658) publishes through its own committer (proposal 0016 rules out reusing
+/// the leased one), so it must call this explicitly; it does not inherit it. Repair commits
+/// ([`commit_chunk_map`], reconstruction, backfill, rebalance) re-commit the same content and
+/// keep preserving `modified` unchanged instead.
+pub fn overwrite_modified(prior: Option<u64>, published: Option<u64>) -> Option<u64> {
+    let published = published?;
+    let Some(prior) = prior else {
+        return Some(published);
+    };
+    let lead = prior.saturating_sub(published);
+    if lead > MODIFIED_MAX_LEAD_MILLIS {
+        tracing::warn!(
+            target: "wyrd.metadata.modified",
+            prior,
+            published,
+            lead_millis = lead,
+            "an overwrite's prior Last-Modified is more than a day ahead of the publishing \
+             clock: one of the two clocks is wrong, and the publishing clock's reading is \
+             recorded instead of ordering after the prior",
+        );
+        return Some(published);
+    }
+    let next_unit = (prior / MODIFIED_WIRE_UNIT_MILLIS)
+        .saturating_add(1)
+        .saturating_mul(MODIFIED_WIRE_UNIT_MILLIS);
+    Some(published.max(next_unit))
+}
